    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is the second most common cancer, with an annual global incidence in excess of 1 million. To identify recurrent genomic aberrations that underlie the development of this malignancy, single nucleotide variant (SNV) determinations from whole-exome sequencing of 12 SCC-normal pairs was used to distill a list of 336 candidate genes that were then sequenced in 100 SCC-matched normal pairs with an average depth exceeding 1200X. KNSTRN ranked third behind CDKN2A and TP53 among recurrently mutated genes in SCC after normalizing for gene length. KNSTRN encodes a kinetochore protein that has not previously been implicated in cancer. Somatic mutations in KNSTRN were present in 2 of 12 (17%) and 19 of 100 (19%) SCCs analyzed by whole exome and targeted sequencing respectively, and most mutations occurred around a UV-signature hotspot serine to phenylalanine substitution present at codon 24 (S24F). KNSTRN S24F was detected in SCC precursors, consistent with an early event in tumorigenesis. Cancer-associated mutant KNSTRN disrupted chromatid cohesion in normal cells, and using whole-genome copy number analysis, SCCs with KNSTRN S24F were determined to be significantly associated with an increase in copy number aberrations. Mutant KNSTRN also enhanced Ras-driven tumorigenesis in vivo. Thus, we report the first cancer-associated missense mutations in the kinetochore gene KNSTRN with dominant, pro-tumorigenic consequences in cutaneous SCC.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
